Q: Java - Free memory of FloatBuffers A: <p>Unfortuntately this question is typically responded to with a version of 'don't bother' instead of the answer. There are indeed situations where you will want to free the FloatBuffer memory manually, like if reloading a large one as I often have to do in my OpenGL mobile game, to avoid an out of memory crash. I've found out the hard way Java doesn't check for eligible garbage collection of unreferenced data to free the memory first before just killing your program.</p>
<pre><code>largefloatbuffer = null;//not .clear()
Runtime.getRuntime().gc();
largefloatbuffer  = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(newlength*mBytesPerFloat);
largefloatbuffer.put(...new large data...);
//'out of memory' crash without the gc()
</code></pre>
